## Rules
- **COVERAGE IS MANDATORY: every numbered Zephyr step above MUST be covered by at least
  one sequence entry.** The objective is only tested if every source step it rests on is
  exercised — a source step with no sequence entry means that part of the objective is
  silently untested. Before you output, check the source-step numbers 1..{{ steps|length }}
  and confirm each appears as some entry's `zephyr_step_idx`. Dropping one is a FAILURE,
  not a simplification.
- **Merging must not lose coverage.** You may merge redundant steps, but a merged entry
  still covers only ONE `zephyr_step_idx`, so do NOT merge steps from different source
  steps — split them instead. A source step describing several COMBINATIONS (e.g. "test
  A-to-B and A-to-C, confirm success in compatible cases and failure in incompatible
  ones") needs ONE ENTRY PER COMBINATION, including the negative/failure cases — those
  are the point of the test, not optional extras.
- Do not downgrade an automatable action into an operator prompt just because a link
  partner is involved. Configuring the partner over its own CLI is automatable; reserve
  `physical`/`manual` for things a human must physically do (reseat a cable, insert a
  pluggable, read an LED).
- Each entry = one concrete action + its verification.
- Split compound steps; merge redundant ones.
- **Output the steps in RUNNABLE EXECUTION ORDER** — the order they must actually run on the device, not necessarily the order they appear above. Setup/configuration must precede the verifications that depend on it; dependencies come first. Manual test cases are often written for a human reader, not for a machine, so reordering is expected.
- "action" = imperative device-level instruction (configure X, send Y, power-cycle, read 'show ...').
- "verify" = the observable check proving it worked (exact CLI fields, packet fields, link state) — becomes self.passed()/self.failed() logic.
{%- if cli_reference %}
  **Quote CLI output only as the REAL CLI REFERENCE below shows it.** Real `show interface`
  prints prose (`current duplex full, current speed 1000, current polarity mdix`);
  `show interface status` prints aligned columns (`a-full`, `a-1000`, `connected`).
  Machine-style tokens like `speed=1000`, `duplex=full` or `state=up` DO NOT EXIST — a
  verify written that way produces a test that fails on working hardware. If the
  reference does not show the field you need, describe the check in plain words instead
  of inventing a format.
- **Verify the FEATURE UNDER TEST, not just that the link survived.** When a step
  enables or disables a feature, the check must read that feature's own state. `show
  interface` reports link/duplex/speed/polarity, so asserting only `Link is UP` after
  disabling a feature passes whenever the disable SILENTLY FAILS — the exact false green
  this rule exists to stop. Use the feature's own show command when the reference lists
  one (e.g. EcoMode/LPI/EEE → `show ecofriendly`, whose per-port `Configured`/`Status`
  columns prove the state). Link stability may be an ADDITIONAL assertion, never the only
  one.
  **`Configured` reflects the CONFIG you applied; `Status` reflects what the port is
  doing now.** So after enabling, `Configured` must read the feature (`lpi`); after
  disabling with the `no` form, `Configured` must read `off` — asserting the feature is
  still `Configured` after disabling it contradicts the very thing the step verifies.
  `Status` may legitimately lag `Configured` (a port can read `Configured=lpi,
  Status=off` when the peer has not negotiated it), so assert `Configured` for "did the
  command take" and `Status` only when the step is about the operational state.
- **Every command ARGUMENT must come from the reference's syntax line.** `speed` accepts
  only `{10|100|1000|2500|5000|10000|40000|100000}` — a value outside that set (e.g.
  `speed 2000`) is rejected by the CLI parser, so the port is never configured and the
  step tests nothing. When a step calls for an UNSUPPORTED or mismatched setting, pick a
  REAL value the hardware may not support on that media (e.g. `2500` or `10000` on a
  1G copper port), or force a mismatch against the partner — never an invented number.
{%- endif %}
- "zephyr_step_idx" = 1-based index of the source Zephyr step above (which manual step this came from). Always set it so the reordering is traceable; use the closest source step when merging or gap-filling.
- **"kind"** = classify EVERY step as exactly one of:
  - `setup` — configuration or precondition with no pass/fail check of its own (base config, enabling a feature, addressing). Becomes suite `configure()`, not a test case.
  - `verify` — a normal automatable action + observable check driven over the CLI/traffic (the common case, the default).
  - `physical` — REQUIRES a human to physically act on the hardware (insert/remove a pluggable, plug/unplug a cable, hot-swap), then the script waits for the resulting port state change (link up/down, pluggable detected) and continues. These are IN SCOPE — do NOT skip them. Model: prompt the operator, then poll `show interface <port> status` for the state transition (see the SVT 3009 Pluggable Qualifications `waitForReplugEvent`/`waitForHotswapEvent` pattern).
  - `manual` — a visual/physical check the DEVICE CANNOT self-report (LED colour, physical seating), needing operator Y/N confirmation (`yesNo("Press Y when …")`).
- For `physical`/`manual` steps, still write the action (the human instruction) and verify (the state change / confirmation to check) — they become real test cases with the interactive pattern, not skips.
- If the execution order differs from the manual step order (reordering, splitting, or merging), state briefly in "notes" what you changed and why.
- Output JSON ONLY, this shape:
{"sequence": [{"n": 1, "action": "...", "verify": "...", "kind": "verify", "zephyr_step_idx": 1}], "notes": "anything assumed or reordered"}
